---

<Base>
  <section class="hero" id="top">
    <img class="hero-media" src={img.park} alt="Crow Wing State Park — the river meeting south of Brainerd" />
    <div class="hero-copy wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow reveal">A history of the crossing</p>
      <h1 class="reveal delay-1">Railroad town. Lake town. Race town.</h1>
      <p class="lede reveal delay-2">Before the shops and the resorts there was a Gull Lake village and a name the maps still carry: Bagone-giizhig — Hole-in-the-Day. Brainerd stayed because the chain turned a weekend into a century of family cabins, a talking giant on a farm, a racetrack in the pines, and a city that appears on the ice every January.</p>
      <p class="scroll-cue">Scroll the century</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="marquee" aria-hidden="true">
    <div class="marquee-track">{[...years, ...years].map((y) => <span>{y} *</span>)}</div>
  </div>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="origins">
    <span class="chapter-num">01</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Chapter I</p>
        <h2>A river already named</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The land that became Brainerd was Anishinaabe — Ojibwe — territory long before a railroad needed a crossing. Woodland peoples left burial mounds at what is now the Gull Lake Dam. Ojibwe families took wild rice, maple sugar, and fish from the chain the maps would later sell as vacation country.</p>
          <p>The Ojibwe name for Gull is remembered as <em>Ga-gaiashkonzikag sagaiigun</em> — the lake of the young gulls. Crow Wing Village, about ten miles south at the meeting of the Crow Wing and the Mississippi, was the earlier town: traders, mixed-blood families, a Catholic mission, and the agency that treated with the Mississippi band.</p>
          <p>Zebulon Pike camped near the future townsite on Christmas Day, 1805. What changed the map was not discovery. It was a railroad that needed to cross the river north of Crow Wing.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.park} alt="Crow Wing State Park at the meeting of two rivers" />
        <figcaption class="caption">Crow Wing State Park — the older meeting place, ten miles south of the shops.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="hole">
    <span class="chapter-num">02</span>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Bagone-giizhig · Gull Lake</p>
      <h2>Hole-in-the-Day</h2>
      <div class="prose" style="max-width:46rem">
        <p>The English name on the bay is a rough translation of Bagone-giizhig — also rendered Hole-in-the-Sky. There were two chiefs of that name, father and son. The bay, a small nearby lake, and a road by Nisswa remember them. Most people meet the name now as a place to drill ice in January.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:2.2rem">
        <article class="card">
          <img class="card-photo face" src={img.holeDag} alt="Daguerreotype of Hole-in-the-Day, 1855, wearing three peace medals" />
          <p class="meta">The Elder · c. 1800–1847</p>
          <h3>A village on Gull</h3>
          <p>Bagone-giizhig the Elder was born on Madeline Island. Around 1836 he moved his band from Sandy Lake to Gull Lake and founded the Mississippi Ojibwe community the treaties later treated as central. Families kept a sugar bush between Gull, Round, and North Long. In May 1847 he fell from a wagon at Little Falls. The wheel crushed his chest. The name passed to his son.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <img class="card-photo face" src={img.hole} alt="Joel E. Whitney carte de visite of Bagone-giizhig the Younger" />
          <p class="meta">The Younger · c. 1825–1868</p>
          <h3>Gwiiwizens, then the name</h3>
          <p>Born Gwiiwizens — Boy. At Fond du Lac in 1847 he claimed to be first chief of all Minnesota Ojibwe. He sat in the 1855 and 1867 treaties, kept a house on Gull, and told people not to move to White Earth until the promises were built. On June 27, 1868, he was shot on the road toward Crow Wing.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="rails">
    <span class="chapter-num">03</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.shops} alt="Northern Pacific Railroad Shops Historic District in Brainerd" />
        <figcaption class="caption">The NP shops — by the 1920s, nine in ten households lived off this paycheck.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Chapter III · 1870</p>
        <h2>Named in a boardroom</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>Northern Pacific president John Gregory Smith chose a crossing seven miles north of Crow Wing. On October 1, 1870, directors named it Brainerd. The wooden trestle opened March 8, 1871. By the 1920s nine in ten households lived off the railroad shops. When the shops shrank, the lakes — and a talking lumberjack — had to become the payroll.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap" style="margin-top:2.6rem">
      <div class="grid-3">
        <figure class="photo" style="min-height:220px">
          <img src={img.roundhouse} alt="Northern Pacific roundhouse at Brainerd" style="min-height:220px" />
          <figcaption class="caption">The roundhouse — locomotives turned and slept here.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="photo" style="min-height:220px">
          <img src={img.bridge} alt="Northern Pacific headquarters and wooden railroad bridge at Brainerd" style="min-height:220px" />
          <figcaption class="caption">Headquarters and the wooden Mississippi bridge, 1870s.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="photo" style="min-height:220px">
          <img src={img.downtown} alt="Aerial view of Brainerd in 1922" style="min-height:220px" />
          <figcaption class="caption">Brainerd from the air, 1922 — shops still at the center.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="film" aria-label="Archive photographs of Brainerd">
    <figure>
      <img src={img.tower} alt="Brainerd historic concrete water tower" />
      <figcaption class="caption">The 1918–21 water tower — first of its kind in the U.S.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src={img.locos} alt="Two Northern Pacific locomotives near Brainerd" />
      <figcaption class="caption">NP power near the crossing</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img class="face" src={img.paulBaxter} alt="John Margolies photograph of the talking Paul Bunyan at the Baxter amusement park, 2003" />
      <figcaption class="caption">Paul at the Baxter corner, 2003 — the year he moved</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src={img.library} alt="Brainerd Carnegie Library" />
      <figcaption class="caption">Carnegie Library, 206 N 7th — opened 1904</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src={img.court} alt="Historic Crow Wing County Courthouse in downtown Brainerd" />
      <figcaption class="caption">Crow Wing County Courthouse</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure>
      <img src={img.paulFarm} alt="Paul Bunyan Land at This Old Farm east of Brainerd" />
      <figcaption class="caption">This Old Farm after the 2003 move</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="lakes">
    <span class="chapter-num">04</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">The chain</p>
        <h2>Gull Lake is why people stay</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>More than 460 lakes sit within 25 miles of Brainerd. The one that made the letterhead is Gull — about 9,947 acres, 80 feet at the deepest hole, 38 miles of shore, chained to a 13,000-acre system.</p>
          <p>Hole-in-the-Day Bay keeps the Younger’s English name. In summer it is docks and golf. In January, if the ice will hold, it becomes a temporary city of 10,000 anglers.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.dock} alt="Pine-ringed northern lake with a wooden dock" />
        <figcaption class="caption">The look the resorts sold: water folded into the pines.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="resorts">
    <span class="chapter-num">05</span>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Neighbors on the peninsula</p>
      <h2>The Maddens and the Craguns</h2>
      <div class="prose" style="max-width:46rem">
        <p>Two University of Minnesota advertising-desk friends — Jack Madden and Merrill Cragun Sr. — helped invent Brainerd as a vacation. In 1936 they put a giant lumberjack in a parade. Then they built resorts within waving distance on Gull Lake.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:2.4rem">
        <article class="card">
          <img class="card-photo" src={img.golf} alt="Golf fairway through trees" />
          <p class="meta">Madden's on Gull Lake · since 1929</p>
          <h3>Nothing down, ten years to pay</h3>
          <p>Jack Madden leased a failing Depression golf course in 1932 for $500, then bought it with nothing down. Fourth generation still runs the same 1,000-acre peninsula.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <img class="card-photo" src={img.lake} alt="Northern lake at dusk" />
          <p class="meta">Cragun's Resort · 1940–2025 in the family</p>
          <h3>A swamp, a gamble</h3>
          <p>Merrill and Louise bought seven acres on Steamboat Bay in 1940. Dutch took over in 1957 and built 54 holes. In 2025, at 93, he sold to Leisure Hotels of Baxter.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="paul">
    <span class="chapter-num">06</span>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Paul Bunyan Land · 1950 / 2003</p>
      <h2>The giant who moved to the farm</h2>
      <div class="prose" style="max-width:46rem">
        <p>The talking Paul at Brainerd did not start as a local myth in wood. He started as a railroad exhibit. After two summers at the Chicago Railroad Fair, 1948 and 1949, Brainerd businessmen J. S. “Sherm” Levis and Roy Kuemicheal bought the 26-foot mechanized lumberjack. Oscar Larson hauled him north in pieces and bolted him together. On July 30, 1950, the city dedicated him at the junction of Highways 210 and 371 — then a two-lane edge of town, now the Kohl’s corner in Baxter — with a parade and fireworks.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:2.4rem">
        <article class="card">
          <img class="card-photo face" src={img.paulBaxter} alt="Talking Paul Bunyan statue at the Baxter amusement park in 2003, photographed by John Margolies" />
          <p class="meta">The Baxter years</p>
          <h3>Hello, and your name</h3>
          <p>Paul Bunyan Center — later the Amusement Center, later the Land — grew up around that stump. Size-80 boots. A 500-pound ax. A belt twelve and a half feet around. Babe the Blue Ox joined him; Sport the Reversible Dog too. In 1978 he got a new head and shirt so he could blink, move his mouth, and raise a hand. A hidden operator learned first names and hometowns. Kids either ran toward him or would not go near.</p>
          <p>By 1963 the twenty-week season drew about 200,000 people. Holiday, Redbook, and National Geographic printed the place. Don and Patti McFarland later ran it — Patti’s family had owned the corner. Attendance slid. In 2003 they announced a close and an auction. Governor Pawlenty said losing Paul and Babe would be “a kick in the cultural groin.” Buyers as far as South Dakota wanted the statue. The old neighborhood was slated for a department store.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="card">
          <img class="card-photo" src={img.paulFarm} alt="Paul Bunyan Land after the move to This Old Farm" />
          <p class="meta">This Old Farm · Highway 18</p>
          <h3>They went for a train</h3>
          <p>Dick Rademacher had been collecting since the 1950s and had turned the family farm east of Brainerd into This Old Farm — a pioneer village of moved buildings and antique iron on about 160 acres. His son Al went to McFarland in 2003 to buy a train and one building for the village. He walked out having bought the amusement park. Sister Lois Moon signed on before the ink dried.</p>
          <p>On September 19, 2003, they laid Paul on his back and trailered him six or seven miles out State Highway 18. Babe, Sport, the rides, the cookshack with the old Cedric Adams narration — the whole carnival followed. The park reopened Memorial Day weekend 2004 and did not miss a season. Dick died in April 2024. The farm still holds the giant.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="wrap split" style="margin-top:3rem">
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.paulSouth} alt="Paul Bunyan statue south of Brainerd after the farm move" />
        <figcaption class="caption">The same giant, farther east — Highway 18.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Pioneer Village · Disney leftover</p>
        <h2>A depot from Iron Will</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>The village is Dick’s other monument: forty-odd buildings, a post office, school, print shop, saloon, sawmill, blacksmith, the oldest homestead in Brainerd — the house that used to stand where the YMCA is now. Shelves of Paramount Theatre paper. A 1953 Buick Roadmaster. Family weddings in the church.</p>
          <p>And then the movie leftovers. Walt Disney Pictures shot <em>Iron Will</em> in the winter of 1993 around Duluth and the North Shore — Mackenzie Astin, Kevin Spacey, a 1917 dog-sled race that never quite happened that way. Crews built a little train-stop town. After wrap, the Birch Ridge depot — the set building from that picture — was moved to This Old Farm and reconstructed in the village. A car from the film sat with it. Not Anaheim. A Disney shed on a Crow Wing County farm, next to a talking lumberjack who used to work for the Chicago railroad fair.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <div class="stat-row">
        <div class="stat"><b>26 ft</b><span>Talking Paul, sitting down</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>1950</b><span>Dedicated at 210 &amp; 371</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>2003</b><span>Moved to the farm</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>1994</b><span>Iron Will depot lands here</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="bir">
    <span class="chapter-num">07</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Donnybrooke · 1968</p>
        <h2>A racetrack in the jack pine</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>George Montgomery cut a 3.1-mile course south of North Long Lake in 1968 and named it Donnybrooke. Jerry Hansen bought the foreclosed track in 1973 and renamed it Brainerd International Raceway. Paul Newman set a lap record here in 1977 and won his first professional race on this pavement in 1982 — the year 50,000 fans came for the first NHRA nationals. The Copham family bought the track in 2006 so the pines would not become lots.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.bir} alt="NHRA drag racing at Brainerd International Raceway, 2017 Lucas Oil Nationals" />
        <figcaption class="caption">Lucas Oil NHRA Nationals at BIR, 2017 — same property as Donnybrooke.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
    <div class="wrap" style="margin-top:2.2rem">
      <figure class="photo" style="min-height:280px">
        <img src={img.birOld} alt="Brainerd International Raceway in May 1997" style="min-height:280px" />
        <figcaption class="caption">BIR in May 1997 — road course in the pines before the next generation of grandstands.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="ice">
    <span class="chapter-num">08</span>
    <div class="wrap split">
      <figure class="photo">
        <img src={img.ice} alt="Frozen northern lake under winter sky" />
        <figcaption class="caption">January weather on a Crow Wing County lake — the Extravaganza itself is still mostly private snapshots.</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div>
        <p class="kicker">Gull Lake · every January</p>
        <h2>The Ice Fishing Extravaganza</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>In 1990 Brainerd Jaycees sketched a contest on a beer case. The first running was January 1991. Volunteers drill thousands of holes on Hole-in-the-Day Bay. More than 10,000 anglers walk out. Most of the money goes to Confidence Learning Center. When Gull is too thin, the contest jumps to Round Lake or runs a hybrid.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="events">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">The years people retell</p>
      <h2>From the bay to the farm</h2>
      <div class="grid-2">
        {events.map(([meta, title, text]) => (
          <article class="card"><p class="meta">{meta}</p><h3>{title}</h3><p>{text}</p></article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter" id="people">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Faces of the crossing</p>
      <h2>People the town remembers</h2>
      <div class="grid-3">
        {people.map((p) => (
          <article class="card">
            {p.photo && <img class="card-photo face" src={p.photo} alt={p.name} />}
            <p class="meta">{p.role}</p>
            <h3>{p.name}</h3>
            <p>{p.text}</p>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter paper" id="timeline">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">A longer century</p>
      <h2>Timeline</h2>
      <div class="timeline">
        {timeline.map(([year, title, text]) => (
          <div class="t-item"><div class="year">{year}</div><div><h3>{title}</h3><p class="prose">{text}</p></div></div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="kicker">Still here</p>
      <h2>What remains</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The bay still says Hole-in-the-Day. Paul still says your name, just farther east, on a farm that also keeps a Disney depot. Madden's is still a family peninsula. BIR still shakes the pines in August. Every winter the Jaycees still ask the ice if it will hold.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat-row">
        <div class="stat"><b>1950</b><span>Paul sits down in Baxter</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>2003</b><span>The farm takes the carnival</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>1986</b><span>Doud, Teacher of the Year</span></div>
        <div class="stat"><b>10k+</b><span>Anglers on the bay in January</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

